# Connection loss and recovery

Say which connection failed. The browser’s connection to Home Assistant, an integration’s connection and an entity’s missing state are three different problems. One unavailable entity must not turn the whole application into an offline screen. Keep retained readings visible only with their age and a note that they may be out of date.

These examples are simulated with invented data. They do not call Home Assistant or control a home. This page covers the design contract for gap HA-GAP-018. It does not add a connection handler.

## Scope and native ownership

The native WebSocket connection authenticates the browser and carries commands, results and event subscriptions. Whether an entity is unknown or unavailable comes from the separate state model. Keep the native connection handling, sign-in and diagnostics. Source: [Home Assistant WebSocket API](https://contributors.home-assistant.io/core/internals/websocket-api/), reviewed on 11 November 2026.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser connection lost | “Home Assistant connection lost”, with the last readings labelled as retained. | Call every device offline. |
| Reconnecting | A persistent status that does not interrupt repeatedly. | Replay a request that changes something. |
| Connection restored | Refresh from the native subscriptions and reconcile pending results. | Treat an open socket as confirmation of an earlier request. |
| One entity unavailable | That the entity cannot supply its state. | Diagnose a Wi-Fi failure without evidence. |
| Sign-in rejected | The native sign-in or re-authentication route. | Collect tokens in a themed error card. |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native connection and subscription lifecycle. Do not open a second device-control connection to support appearance changes.
2. Track a pending request apart from the last reported state. After a lost response the action may have completed, be pending or have been rejected. No timeout can show which.
3. Keep non-secret draft inputs only where the editor really keeps them. Re-authentication and navigation can keep different amounts of data, so describe each accurately.
4. In safe or recovery mode, native controls and branding take precedence. If an adapter target is missing, the adapter must fail quietly and leave the connection messages and the native theme selector in place.

## Accessible interaction

- Use a polite persistent status for routine reconnecting and announce a meaningful change once. Do not put the whole updating dashboard in a live region.
- Keep focus on the interrupted task unless sign-in needs a new context. Keep a return path where possible.
- Put recovery text beside a disabled action. Colour and a spinning icon cannot say which task is unavailable.

## Failure and recovery

- A retry link must perform a real retry. Otherwise link to instructions and label it that way.
- After reconnecting, read the current reported state before asking the person to repeat an action that may already have happened.
- Keep failures from native services visible, even when a theme adapter stopped attaching.

